            <ArticleTitle>Piriformis syndrome surgery causing severe sciatic nerve injury.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>45-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/CND.0b013e318259617c</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Piriformis syndrome is a controversial entrapment neuropathy in which the sciatic nerve is thought to be compressed by the piriformis muscle. Two patients developed severe left sciatic neuropathy after piriformis muscle release. One had a total sciatic nerve lesion, whereas the second had a predominantly high common peroneal nerve lesion. Follow-up studies showed reinnervation of the hamstrings only. We conclude that piriformis muscle surgery may be hazardous and result in devastating sciatic nerve injury.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
